<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Two things throw people off before we even open the machine: the font, and CL.</h2>

    <p><strong>On a Samsung washer, the display font makes different-looking codes mean the same thing. The digit 5 and the letter S are drawn identically, the digit 0 and the letter O are drawn identically, and the last letter (C vs E) is only a firmware generation. So <span class="code">5C = 5E = SC = SE</span> are all one drain fault, and <span class="code">4C = 4E</span> are one water-supply fault. Chasing "SE" as if it were separate from "5C" wastes an evening. And <span class="code">CL</span> is not an error at all — it's Child Lock, cleared by holding the lock button combo for about 3 seconds. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Here's the honest version of a Samsung washer code, brand-first. The display flashes something, the wash stops mid-cycle, and the search results argue with each other. Most Samsung faults land in four buckets: a drain fault (the 5C font family, drain pump), a fill fault (4C/4E, inlet valve), an unbalance fault (UE and its cousins, suspension), or a door-lock fault (dE/dL, interlock). Our techs decode the specific code and then confirm on-site, because the same code can trace to a cheap hose or an expensive board depending on what the control logged.</p>

    <p>Below is the font-alias key first, then the full Samsung code table, then the fixes bucket by bucket. This is the same reference our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair carry to Samsung calls across LA County, Orange, Ventura, Santa Barbara, San Bernardino, Riverside, and San Diego. Parent reference: <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">all washer error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/samsung-washer-repair/">Samsung washer repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Font aliases</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same fault, different-looking code. Read this before you Google "SE".</h2>
    <p class="lead">Samsung's segmented display can't tell a 5 from an S or a 0 from an O — they share the same lit segments. And the trailing letter shifted from E on older machines to C on newer ones. That means several "different" codes are one fault.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>What you might see</th><th>All mean</th><th>Fault</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5C · 5E · SC · SE · nd</td><td>Drain fault</td><td>Pump filter, hose, drain pump motor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4C · 4E</td><td>Water-supply / fill fault</td><td>Inlet hose, screen, inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1C · 1E · 1O</td><td>Water-level sensor</td><td>Pressure sensor or its hose</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UE · Ub · UR · Ur · U6</td><td>Unbalanced load</td><td>Load spread, then suspension / sensor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OE · OF · 0C · 0F</td><td>Overflow</td><td>Pressure switch or inlet valve stuck open</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>So if the manual you found only lists "5C" but your machine shows "SE," you're already in the right place — same drain fault, no separate hunt needed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Full code table</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung WF / WW / WA washer codes.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>4C / 4E</td><td>Water supply / fill fault</td><td>Inlet hose, mesh screen, inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5C / 5E / SC / SE / nd</td><td>Drain fault</td><td>Pump filter (DIY), drain hose, pump motor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UE / Ub / UR</td><td>Unbalanced load</td><td>Redistribute load; suspension / sensor if persistent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>dc (lowercase)</td><td>Unbalance (redundant signal)</td><td>Same family as UE — see honest note on dC below</td></tr>
        <tr><td>dE / dL</td><td>Door / door-lock fault</td><td>Door interlock assembly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1C / 1E / LC / LE</td><td>Water-level / leak sensor</td><td>Pressure sensor, or a real leak (door boot, hose)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OE / OF</td><td>Overflow</td><td>Pressure switch or inlet valve stuck open</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tE</td><td>Temperature sensor</td><td>Thermistor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HE</td><td>Heater fault</td><td>Heating element / heater relay (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SUd / Sud</td><td>Excess suds</td><td>Too much or non-HE detergent (customer-side)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AE</td><td>Communication fault</td><td>Board-to-board harness or control board (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PF</td><td>Power failure mid-cycle</td><td>Power interruption; restart cycle</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CL</td><td>Child Lock (not a fault)</td><td>Hold the lock button combo ~3 seconds</td></tr>
