</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Drain fault</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The 5C font family — start at the filter.</h2>
    <p>A 5C, SE, SC, or nd means the washer tried to pump out and couldn't finish in time. On front-load WF and WW machines there's a drain pump filter behind a small access door at the lower front — that's the first stop, and it's customer-cleanable. Lay down a towel, set a shallow pan under it, twist the cap counterclockwise, and pull out the lint, coins, and the occasional hair clip that's jamming the impeller. Roughly half of these calls end right there.</p>
    <p>If the filter's clean and the code comes back, the next suspects are a kinked or clogged drain hose at the standpipe and then the drain pump motor itself. Pump replacement runs $380 to $540 with labor. On WA top-load models there's no front filter — the drain path runs straight to the pump, so those go to a hose-and-pump check on the visit. Related symptom page: <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Fill fault</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">4C / 4E — water isn't arriving fast enough.</h2>
    <p>The washer expected a certain amount of water in a certain window and didn't get it. Work from cheap to expensive: both supply valves fully open, fill hoses not kinked behind the machine, then the little mesh screens where the hoses screw onto the inlet valve. Those screens catch sediment, and in the harder-water pockets across the Inland Empire and the Valley they clog often enough that we clean them on a good share of 4C calls. If the inlet valve solenoid itself failed, it's a $260 to $380 replacement. 4C and 4E are the identical fault — older firmware just printed the E.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Unbalance</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">UE, Ub, UR — when it's the laundry and when it's the machine.</h2>
    <p>An unbalance code on one lopsided load (a single comforter, or three pairs of jeans piled on one side) is the washer protecting itself: it won't ramp to spin speed off-center. Spread the load and it goes away. That's normal, not a fault.</p>
    <p>The tell that it's hardware is when UE fires on <em>balanced</em> loads, including small ones. At that point our techs look at the suspension rods and shock absorbers (worn ones let the tub wander) and at the vibration sensor that reports position to the board. Suspension or shock replacement runs $440 to $640. We check those before we ever tell you to just rearrange the wash, because if it's the rods, no amount of rearranging fixes it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Door &amp; the dC conflict</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">dE and dL are the door. dC is where the sources disagree — so we watch, not guess.</h2>
    <p>dE and dL are door-lock faults: the washer can't confirm the door is latched, so it won't run (a front-loader will not spin with an unlocked door, by design). The usual fix is the door interlock assembly, $260 to $440.</p>
    <p>The messy one is dC. Samsung's own materials aren't consistent across model years — lowercase <span class="code">dc</span> is most often an unbalance signal (same family as UE), while some charts print an uppercase <span class="code">dC</span> for the door. Rather than argue with the letter, our techs read the behavior. Quits during spin and shuffles the load? We chase suspension and the unbalance sensor. Won't start and won't lock at all? We chase the interlock. The machine's action settles what the label leaves ambiguous.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Leak, heater, suds, power</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The rest of the list, sorted by who fixes it.</h2>
    <p><strong>Leak / water-level (1C, 1E, LC, LE):</strong> the level or leak sensor spoke up. Could be a genuine early leak — a loose clamp, a cracked tub-to-pump hose, a worn front-load door boot ($220 to $380) — or a sensor reading wrong with a dry floor. These need an on-site look; they don't clear from a power-cycle if the fault is real.</p>
    <p><strong>Heater (tE, HE):</strong> temperature-sensor and heater-circuit faults. Technician work — there's mains voltage at the element and the diagnosis needs a meter on the thermistor, element, and heater relay.</p>
    <p><strong>Suds (SUd, Sud):</strong> too much detergent or a non-HE soap foaming up. The washer pauses to clear the foam. Cut the dose, switch to HE detergent, and it stops. Customer-side, no visit.</p>
    <p><strong>Communication (AE):</strong> the boards lost their handshake — a harness or the control board. Technician diagnosis.</p>
    <p><strong>Power (PF):</strong> the power blipped mid-cycle. Restart the cycle. If it keeps happening on a circuit that's fine for everything else, then it's worth a look at the machine's power board.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Clear Child Lock (CL).</strong> Hold the two buttons printed under the small padlock icon for about 3 seconds. CL isn't a fault.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug for 60 seconds (or flip the breaker), restore power, run a rinse-and-spin. Clears transient sensor noise.</li>
      <li><strong>Drain pump filter (front-load).</strong> Lower-front access door, pan underneath, twist counterclockwise, clear debris. Fixes a high share of 5C / SE drain codes.</li>
      <li><strong>Inlet hose &amp; screen check.</strong> Valves fully open, hoses not kinked, mesh screens clear of sediment. Resolves many 4C / 4E codes.</li>
      <li><strong>Detergent dose.</strong> For SUd, cut the amount and use HE detergent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Leak (LC/1E), heater (tE/HE), door (dE/dL), and communication (AE) codes don't clear from a customer reset when the fault is real — those are the ones worth a call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A year 8+ Samsung throwing several different codes usually has a dying board.</h2>
    <p>When an older Samsung shows a drain code one week, an unbalance code the next, and a door code after that, the honest read is rarely three broken parts. It's the main control board aging and sending false signals. Year 8 to 12 is the typical window. Board replacement runs $580 to $880, and at that age the smart move is to weigh it against a new machine — we run that math with you at the door instead of quietly selling you a board on a washer that's near the end anyway.</p>
  </div>
</section>

